Jahshaka patch 0038 — enable VK_KHR_acceleration_structure + VK_KHR_ray_query on
the device, so a Jahshaka-owned compute pass can run inline hardware ray queries.
NOTHING IN OGRE USES IT.

WHY (Photon lane R1; SPECS/PHOTON_SPEC.md §7 R1,
SPECS/research/VULKAN_RAY_QUERY_NVIDIA_2026-09-15.md §1,
SPECS/audits/PHOTON_AUDIT_C_2026-09-14.md finding 8; measured by spike PHOTON-S3,
spikes/photon-s3/FINDINGS.md §2). Photon's ray tier traces the ACTUAL triangles —
the one representation that is exact for movers, and the answer to probe
visibility, hard sun contact and (R5) reflections without any notion of a room.
The rays live entirely in an Ogre-private TU of ours (irisgl/engine/src/
OgreRayQuery.cpp) with its own pipeline and descriptor set; Ogre's Hlms,
RootLayout, BarrierSolver and compositor are not involved in the trace. The only
thing the pin must give us is a DEVICE that has the extensions on.

WHAT THE PIN HAS. Nothing: grepping the whole tree for ray_query /
acceleration_structure returns one barrier access-bit constant and SPIR-V enum
values in a vendored header. Three things block the extensions:

 1. The VkInstance is created at `appInfo.apiVersion = VK_MAKE_VERSION( 1, 0, 2 )`
    (OgreVulkanDevice.cpp, ~:291). The instance's apiVersion is the ceiling for
    the whole process, and VK_KHR_acceleration_structure needs Vulkan 1.1 +
    descriptor_indexing + buffer_device_address, or 1.2, plus
    VK_KHR_deferred_host_operations.
 2. Device extensions are a hardcoded if/else whitelist (~:948-973): a name not
    in that chain never reaches vkCreateDevice, whatever the driver advertises.
 3. The feature pNext chain handed to vkCreateDevice is a fixed set of three
    structs, so the acceleration-structure / ray-query / buffer-device-address
    feature bits can never be turned on.

WHY A PATCH AND NOT A HOOK. The pin does accept an externally created instance
and device (`external_instance` / `external_device` in the RS options), and our
engine owns those miscParams — but taking that route means owning physical-device
selection, queue-family selection, the feature fill, swapchain-extension
correctness and present-queue discovery forever, permanently divergent from the
pin and re-validated at every Ogre bump (est. 300-500 lines of ours). This patch
is 135 lines in one render-system file plus its header, re-applied by
build-ogre.sh, and it fails loudly at the next pin bump if upstream moves the
code. A patch beats a workaround (owner, 2026-09-15).

WHAT THIS PATCH DOES.

 (0) THE NO-RAYS SWITCH, HONOURED AT THE DEVICE. With JAHSHAKA_NO_RAY_QUERY set,
     NOTHING below happens: the instance stays at 1.0.2, not one ray extension is
     requested and not one feature bit is set, so the process runs on EXACTLY the
     device it would have had if this patch did not exist. That is what makes the
     switch a real fallback picture rather than a cosmetic one - Studio's
     --no-ray-query and Engine::setRayTracing read the same variable to decide
     whether to build acceleration structures - and it is the reason a suite can
     prove the no-rays path on ray-capable hardware.

     IT IS ALSO A NECESSARY ESCAPE HATCH, measured: on NVIDIA 595.84,
     vkCreateDevice returns VK_ERROR_INITIALIZATION_FAILED with these features
     enabled UNDER AddressSanitizer, on the very GPU where the same call
     succeeds without it (no validation error is reported; the driver simply
     refuses). Every ASan engine suite therefore boots with the variable set and
     gets its historical device back, byte for byte. This is a DRIVER limit, not
     a defect in the patch: the non-ASan build of the same code creates the
     device, builds structures and traces correctly.

 (a) INSTANCE VERSION. Asks vkEnumerateInstanceVersion (itself a Vulkan 1.1 entry
     point) what the loader supports and raises apiVersion to 1.2 only when the
     loader can give it. A 1.0 loader, or one that does not export the function,
     keeps the historical 1.0.2 byte for byte. The chosen version is logged.
     NOTE for macOS (audit C-6): LunarG's loader there reports >= 1.2, so the
     instance DOES go to 1.2 on a MoltenVK box — MoltenVK supports 1.2 and the
     extensions are simply absent, so hunk (c) leaves the tier off; the Mac's
     path is the no-rays path either way. This is recorded, not assumed: the
     deferred Mac session logs the line this hunk prints.

 (b) EXTENSIONS. Seven names join the whitelist, each guarded by the same
     "only if the driver advertises it" rule as every name already there:
     VK_KHR_acceleration_structure, VK_KHR_ray_query,
     VK_KHR_deferred_host_operations, VK_KHR_buffer_device_address,
     VK_EXT_descriptor_indexing, VK_KHR_spirv_1_4, VK_KHR_shader_float_controls.
     The last four are dependencies of the first two (promoted to core in 1.2;
     requested explicitly so the patch is also correct on a 1.1 clamp).

 (c) FEATURES. A VulkanDevice::RayQueryVkFeatures MEMBER (not a local — the chain
     must stay valid until vkCreateDevice reads it, which is after
     fillDeviceFeatures2 has returned) holds the four feature structs, chained
     only when all five required extensions were requested.

     THE FOOT-GUN THIS PATCH MUST KEEP, and every later feature patch must copy:
     upstream uses the SAME pNext chain to QUERY the driver
     (vkGetPhysicalDeviceFeatures2) and to ENABLE features (vkCreateDevice), so
     whatever the query leaves set gets turned on. This patch therefore clears
     every reported bit and puts back exactly three: accelerationStructure,
     rayQuery and bufferDeviceAddress. No capture-replay, no host commands, no
     indirect build, and not one descriptor-indexing feature (the extension is a
     DEPENDENCY of VK_KHR_acceleration_structure; none of its features are
     needed). Without the mask, enabling the tier would quietly change driver
     behaviour for the whole engine.

 (d) `VulkanDevice::hasRayQuery()` — the one accessor our compute pass needs to
     know whether it may build acceleration structures here. Ogre never calls it;
     patch 0039 does.

WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE. Ogre's swapchain, present path, Hlms, RootLayout, shaders
and descriptor sets are untouched; no Ogre code path branches on the instance's
apiVersion (the debug-utils check makes its own vkEnumerateInstanceVersion call,
and properties read deviceProps.apiVersion). On a device without the extensions
every hunk is inert. Measured on the RTX 4080 SUPER, driver 595.84: the app
boots, renders and presents identically, the validation layer reports zero
errors, and --engine-selftest produces the unchanged expected hash with the tier
enabled and no tracing (PHOTON-S3 §2, re-proved by PHOTON-R1).

SOURCE patch: every tree re-runs ./irisgl/scripts/build-ogre.sh.
Companion: 0039 (the VBO pools as BLAS build input) depends on hasRayQuery().

diff --git a/RenderSystems/Vulkan/include/OgreVulkanDevice.h b/RenderSystems/Vulkan/include/OgreVulkanDevice.h
index 5f2f19e1b..10efb0da0 100644
--- a/RenderSystems/Vulkan/include/OgreVulkanDevice.h
+++ b/RenderSystems/Vulkan/include/OgreVulkanDevice.h
@@ -136,6 +136,21 @@ namespace Ogre
             VkBool32 pipelineCreationCacheControl;
         };
 
+        /// Jahshaka (ogre-patch 0038): the feature structs a hardware ray-query tier
+        /// needs at vkCreateDevice time. They are MEMBERS, not locals, because the
+        /// pNext chain they are linked into must outlive fillDeviceFeatures2() and
+        /// stay valid until vkCreateDevice() reads it.
+        struct RayQueryVkFeatures
+        {
+            VkPhysicalDeviceAccelerationStructureFeaturesKHR accelStruct;
+            VkPhysicalDeviceRayQueryFeaturesKHR rayQuery;
+            VkPhysicalDeviceBufferDeviceAddressFeatures bufferDeviceAddress;
+            VkPhysicalDeviceDescriptorIndexingFeatures descriptorIndexing;
+            /// True only when every extension the tier needs was found AND the
+            /// driver reported both accelerationStructure and rayQuery.
+            bool enabled;
+        };
+
         // clang-format off
         std::shared_ptr<VulkanInstance> mInstance;
         VkPhysicalDevice    mPhysicalDevice;
@@ -156,6 +171,7 @@ namespace Ogre
         VkPhysicalDeviceMemoryProperties mDeviceMemoryProperties;
         VkPhysicalDeviceFeatures mDeviceFeatures;
         ExtraVkFeatures mDeviceExtraFeatures;
+        RayQueryVkFeatures mRayQueryFeatures;
         FastArray<VkQueueFamilyProperties> mQueueProps;
 
         /// Extensions requested when created. Sorted
@@ -200,6 +216,13 @@ namespace Ogre
 
         bool hasDeviceExtension( const IdString extension ) const;
 
+        /// Jahshaka (ogre-patch 0038): true when VK_KHR_acceleration_structure +
+        /// VK_KHR_ray_query were requested at vkCreateDevice and the driver reported
+        /// their features. Nothing in Ogre reads this; it is the one honest way for a
+        /// Jahshaka-owned compute pass to know whether it may build acceleration
+        /// structures on this device.
+        bool hasRayQuery() const { return mRayQueryFeatures.enabled; }
+
         void initQueues();
 
         void commitAndNextCommandBuffer(
diff --git a/RenderSystems/Vulkan/src/OgreVulkanDevice.cpp b/RenderSystems/Vulkan/src/OgreVulkanDevice.cpp
index 57d308ea4..d5e3943d0 100644
--- a/RenderSystems/Vulkan/src/OgreVulkanDevice.cpp
+++ b/RenderSystems/Vulkan/src/OgreVulkanDevice.cpp
@@ -58,6 +58,24 @@ THE SOFTWARE.
 
 namespace Ogre
 {
+    /// Jahshaka (ogre-patch 0038): THE NO-RAYS SWITCH, honoured at the DEVICE.
+    /// With it in force nothing this patch adds happens at all - the instance
+    /// stays at 1.0.2, not one ray extension is requested and not one feature
+    /// bit is set - so the process runs on EXACTLY the device it would have had
+    /// if this patch did not exist. That is what makes the switch a real
+    /// fallback picture rather than a cosmetic one (Jahshaka reads the same
+    /// variable to decide whether to build acceleration structures at all).
+    ///
+    /// It is also the escape hatch for a driver that cannot build a ray-tracing
+    /// device in a particular process: MEASURED on NVIDIA 595.84, vkCreateDevice
+    /// returns VK_ERROR_INITIALIZATION_FAILED with these features enabled under
+    /// AddressSanitizer, on the very GPU where the same call succeeds without it.
+    static bool jahNoRayQuery()
+    {
+        static const bool noRays = getenv( "JAHSHAKA_NO_RAY_QUERY" ) != 0;
+        return noRays;
+    }
+
     FastArray<const char *> VulkanInstance::enabledExtensions;  // sorted
     FastArray<const char *> VulkanInstance::enabledLayers;      // sorted
 #if OGRE_DEBUG_MODE >= OGRE_DEBUG_HIGH
@@ -288,7 +306,31 @@ namespace Ogre
                 appInfo.pApplicationName = appName.c_str();
             appInfo.pEngineName = "Ogre3D Vulkan Engine";
             appInfo.engineVersion = OGRE_VERSION;
+            // Jahshaka (ogre-patch 0038): ask for Vulkan 1.2 when the LOADER can give
+            // it. VK_KHR_acceleration_structure and VK_KHR_ray_query are only usable
+            // above 1.0, and the instance's apiVersion is the ceiling for the whole
+            // process. vkEnumerateInstanceVersion is itself a 1.1 entry point, so a
+            // 1.0 loader (or a null return) keeps the historical 1.0.2 exactly.
             appInfo.apiVersion = VK_MAKE_VERSION( 1, 0, 2 );
+            if( !jahNoRayQuery() )
+            {
+                PFN_vkEnumerateInstanceVersion enumerateInstanceVersion =
+                    (PFN_vkEnumerateInstanceVersion)vkGetInstanceProcAddr(
+                        VK_NULL_HANDLE, "vkEnumerateInstanceVersion" );
+                if( enumerateInstanceVersion )
+                {
+                    uint32_t loaderVersion = 0u;
+                    if( enumerateInstanceVersion( &loaderVersion ) == VK_SUCCESS &&
+                        loaderVersion >= VK_API_VERSION_1_2 )
+                    {
+                        appInfo.apiVersion = VK_API_VERSION_1_2;
+                    }
+                }
+            }
+            LogManager::getSingleton().logMessage(
+                "Vulkan: Instance apiVersion " +
+                StringConverter::toString( VK_VERSION_MAJOR( appInfo.apiVersion ) ) + "." +
+                StringConverter::toString( VK_VERSION_MINOR( appInfo.apiVersion ) ) );
 
             VkInstanceCreateInfo createInfo;
             makeVkStruct( createInfo, VK_STRUCTURE_TYPE_INSTANCE_CREATE_INFO );
@@ -666,12 +708,81 @@ namespace Ogre
             lastNext = &deviceCacheControlFeatures.pNext;
         }
 
+        // Jahshaka (ogre-patch 0038): the ray-query feature structs. Chained only
+        // when every extension they belong to was requested; the structs live on the
+        // device object so the chain survives until vkCreateDevice reads it.
+        memset( &mRayQueryFeatures, 0, sizeof( mRayQueryFeatures ) );
+        makeVkStruct( mRayQueryFeatures.accelStruct,
+                      VK_STRUCTURE_TYPE_PHYSICAL_DEVICE_ACCELERATION_STRUCTURE_FEATURES_KHR );
+        makeVkStruct( mRayQueryFeatures.rayQuery,
+                      VK_STRUCTURE_TYPE_PHYSICAL_DEVICE_RAY_QUERY_FEATURES_KHR );
+        makeVkStruct( mRayQueryFeatures.bufferDeviceAddress,
+                      VK_STRUCTURE_TYPE_PHYSICAL_DEVICE_BUFFER_DEVICE_ADDRESS_FEATURES );
+        makeVkStruct( mRayQueryFeatures.descriptorIndexing,
+                      VK_STRUCTURE_TYPE_PHYSICAL_DEVICE_DESCRIPTOR_INDEXING_FEATURES );
+        const bool bWantRayQuery =
+            !jahNoRayQuery() &&
+            hasDeviceExtension( VK_KHR_ACCELERATION_STRUCTURE_EXTENSION_NAME ) &&
+            hasDeviceExtension( VK_KHR_RAY_QUERY_EXTENSION_NAME ) &&
+            hasDeviceExtension( VK_KHR_DEFERRED_HOST_OPERATIONS_EXTENSION_NAME ) &&
+            hasDeviceExtension( VK_KHR_BUFFER_DEVICE_ADDRESS_EXTENSION_NAME ) &&
+            hasDeviceExtension( VK_EXT_DESCRIPTOR_INDEXING_EXTENSION_NAME );
+        if( bWantRayQuery )
+        {
+            *lastNext = &mRayQueryFeatures.accelStruct;
+            lastNext = &mRayQueryFeatures.accelStruct.pNext;
+            *lastNext = &mRayQueryFeatures.rayQuery;
+            lastNext = &mRayQueryFeatures.rayQuery.pNext;
+            *lastNext = &mRayQueryFeatures.bufferDeviceAddress;
+            lastNext = &mRayQueryFeatures.bufferDeviceAddress.pNext;
+            *lastNext = &mRayQueryFeatures.descriptorIndexing;
+            lastNext = &mRayQueryFeatures.descriptorIndexing.pNext;
+        }
+
         GetPhysicalDeviceFeatures2KHR( mPhysicalDevice, &deviceFeatures2 );
         mDeviceExtraFeatures.storageInputOutput16 = device16BitStorageFeatures.storageInputOutput16;
         mDeviceExtraFeatures.shaderFloat16 = deviceShaderFloat16Int8Features.shaderFloat16;
         mDeviceExtraFeatures.shaderInt8 = deviceShaderFloat16Int8Features.shaderInt8;
         mDeviceExtraFeatures.pipelineCreationCacheControl =
             deviceCacheControlFeatures.pipelineCreationCacheControl;
+
+        // Jahshaka (ogre-patch 0038): the query above FILLED these structs with
+        // everything the driver can do, and this same chain is handed straight to
+        // vkCreateDevice - so anything left set here is ENABLED. Keep exactly the
+        // four bits a ray-query tier needs and clear the rest, so enabling the tier
+        // cannot quietly change driver behaviour for the rest of the engine. The
+        // descriptor-indexing struct is chained but left entirely off: the extension
+        // is a dependency of VK_KHR_acceleration_structure, none of its features are.
+        mRayQueryFeatures.enabled =
+            bWantRayQuery && mRayQueryFeatures.accelStruct.accelerationStructure &&
+            mRayQueryFeatures.rayQuery.rayQuery &&
+            mRayQueryFeatures.bufferDeviceAddress.bufferDeviceAddress;
+        {
+            // Clear every reported bit, then put back only the ones we use. sType and
+            // pNext are restored so the chain vkCreateDevice is about to walk survives.
+            auto clearBody = []( auto &featureStruct ) {
+                const VkStructureType sType = featureStruct.sType;
+                void *const pNext = featureStruct.pNext;
+                memset( &featureStruct, 0, sizeof( featureStruct ) );
+                featureStruct.sType = sType;
+                featureStruct.pNext = pNext;
+            };
+            clearBody( mRayQueryFeatures.accelStruct );
+            clearBody( mRayQueryFeatures.rayQuery );
+            clearBody( mRayQueryFeatures.bufferDeviceAddress );
+            clearBody( mRayQueryFeatures.descriptorIndexing );
+            if( mRayQueryFeatures.enabled )
+            {
+                mRayQueryFeatures.accelStruct.accelerationStructure = VK_TRUE;
+                mRayQueryFeatures.rayQuery.rayQuery = VK_TRUE;
+                mRayQueryFeatures.bufferDeviceAddress.bufferDeviceAddress = VK_TRUE;
+            }
+            LogManager::getSingleton().logMessage(
+                String( "Vulkan: hardware ray query " ) +
+                ( mRayQueryFeatures.enabled
+                      ? "AVAILABLE (VK_KHR_acceleration_structure + VK_KHR_ray_query enabled)"
+                      : "not available" ) );
+        }
         return true;
     }
     //-------------------------------------------------------------------------
@@ -970,6 +1081,33 @@ namespace Ogre
             // purpose: the name macro lives behind VK_ENABLE_BETA_EXTENSIONS.
             else if( extensionName == "VK_KHR_portability_subset" )
                 deviceExtensions.push_back( "VK_KHR_portability_subset" );
+            // Jahshaka (ogre-patch 0038): the hardware ray-query set. Every one of
+            // these is requested ONLY if the driver advertises it, exactly like the
+            // names above; on a device without them nothing changes. Ogre itself uses
+            // none of them - they exist so a Jahshaka-owned compute pass can build
+            // acceleration structures and run inline ray queries.
+            // Each arm pushes the MACRO, which is a string literal with static
+            // storage - never extensionName.c_str(), which dies with this loop
+            // iteration while deviceExtensions keeps the pointer.
+            else if( !jahNoRayQuery() &&
+                     extensionName == VK_KHR_ACCELERATION_STRUCTURE_EXTENSION_NAME )
+                deviceExtensions.push_back( VK_KHR_ACCELERATION_STRUCTURE_EXTENSION_NAME );
+            else if( !jahNoRayQuery() && extensionName == VK_KHR_RAY_QUERY_EXTENSION_NAME )
+                deviceExtensions.push_back( VK_KHR_RAY_QUERY_EXTENSION_NAME );
+            else if( !jahNoRayQuery() &&
+                     extensionName == VK_KHR_DEFERRED_HOST_OPERATIONS_EXTENSION_NAME )
+                deviceExtensions.push_back( VK_KHR_DEFERRED_HOST_OPERATIONS_EXTENSION_NAME );
+            else if( !jahNoRayQuery() &&
+                     extensionName == VK_KHR_BUFFER_DEVICE_ADDRESS_EXTENSION_NAME )
+                deviceExtensions.push_back( VK_KHR_BUFFER_DEVICE_ADDRESS_EXTENSION_NAME );
+            else if( !jahNoRayQuery() &&
+                     extensionName == VK_EXT_DESCRIPTOR_INDEXING_EXTENSION_NAME )
+                deviceExtensions.push_back( VK_EXT_DESCRIPTOR_INDEXING_EXTENSION_NAME );
+            else if( !jahNoRayQuery() && extensionName == VK_KHR_SPIRV_1_4_EXTENSION_NAME )
+                deviceExtensions.push_back( VK_KHR_SPIRV_1_4_EXTENSION_NAME );
+            else if( !jahNoRayQuery() &&
+                     extensionName == VK_KHR_SHADER_FLOAT_CONTROLS_EXTENSION_NAME )
+                deviceExtensions.push_back( VK_KHR_SHADER_FLOAT_CONTROLS_EXTENSION_NAME );
         }
 
         deviceExtensions.push_back( VK_KHR_SWAPCHAIN_EXTENSION_NAME );
